Remove the BLE transport and the bandwidth upgrade

Upstream's default `experimental` feature adds a whole Bluetooth LE
transport: a BLE listener and advertiser (btleplug), a GATT server and an
L2CAP server that take connections from phones (bluer, over BlueZ's own
D-Bus bindings), BLE recipient discovery and dialling, the "migratable"
stream that lets a connection start on BLE and move to TCP, and the
bandwidth-upgrade negotiation (BWU) that moves it: offering our own
address, accepting a second connection, draining the first, answering
AUTO_RESUME, and, when there is no shared LAN, hosting or joining a
Wi-Fi network through NetworkManager (Q6, next patch).

Sukkula uses Quick Share over the Wi-Fi LAN only (spec F-QS1: mDNS and
TCP); its only BLE use is the advertisement that makes phones start their
mDNS announcement (F-QS2), which it sends itself through BlueZ over the
system libdbus. None of the transport is used, it is several thousand
lines of connection handling that a phone in Bluetooth range could reach
before any consent, it pulls in a second and third D-Bus stack (bluer's
and btleplug's), and the BWU code is what drives the network changes of
Q6. A Quick Share connection that starts on TCP has nothing to upgrade
to, so every BWU path goes with it.

Removed: src/hdl/{ble,blea,gatt,l2cap,migratable,ble_client}.rs, the
`experimental` feature and the bluer, btleplug, futures and uuid
dependencies; the BLE blocks in lib.rs (RQS::run no longer returns the
"nearby device is sharing" channel, which only the BLE listener fed) and
in manager.rs (SendInfo::ble, the BLE send path); in the inbound and
outbound handlers the upgrade state, the BWU and AUTO_RESUME frame
handling and the MigratableStream impls; the "a phone nearby is sharing"
re-announcement in mdns.rs, and EndpointInfo's BLE address and PSM
fields. Outgoing connections announce WIFI_LAN as their only medium.
hotspot.rs is no longer referenced; the next patch deletes it.

Findings: Q6 (spec §5), F-QS1, F-QS2. Report: docs/UPSTREAM-QUICKSHARE.md Q6.
